Thouret’s committee proposed recognizing as voters (“active citizens”) adult French men who:

- Were French-born or naturalized;
- Were at least 25 years old;
- Had lived in their canton for at least one year;
- Paid direct taxes equal to at least three days’ local wages; and
- Were not servants.

Women, minors, servants, and others deprived of political rights were excluded.

Nevada’s Pregnant Workers’ Fairness Act protects female employees and job applicants with conditions related to pregnancy, childbirth, recovery, or associated medical conditions—including lactation and pregnancy loss.

It safeguards them against employers:

- Refusing reasonable accommodations;
- Taking adverse action or denying employment opportunities because they requested or used an accommodation;
- Forcing them to accept an accommodation they did not request;
- Requiring leave when a reasonable accommodation would allow them to work; and
- Refusing to reinstate them to the same or an equivalent position after leave.

The Act generally applies to employers with 15 or more employees, subject to limited contractor exceptions.
